            <ArticleTitle>Computed tomography findings of primary epiploic appendagitis as an easily misdiagnosed entity: Case series and review of literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>489-494</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5505/tjtes.2017.99894</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Primer epiploic appendagitis (PEA) is an uncommon condition.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We retrospectively reviewed the clinical records and computed tomography (CT) findings of 45 patients with PEA.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">On the basis of physical examination and pain localization, presumptive clinical diagnosis was acute appendicitis (n=13), acute cholecystitis (n=2), acute diverticulitis (n=19), renal colic (n=7) and ovarian pathology (n=4).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although it has no characteristic clinical and laboratory features, CT is the best modality for accurate diagnosis of PEA.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
